>> Little more risk than Ludious or the quest with a higher reward.

I'm not sure why people are *so* squeamish about tackling Ludios without
reflection. Sure, there are a lot of soldiers there, and sure, a
selected few of them may carry a wand. In my experience, Ludios will
have at most four wands total, usually two or three. The chance that one
of these is death is almost neglectable, and if one of them is, there's
usually a soldier between you and the one wielding the wand, so you get
an early warning. If not, there's always your armour, and then you have
time to retreat.

> I actually tried it.  But as soon as I set foot on the castle level,
> an arch-lich casually strolled up to me.  I turned tail and ran.

Wise thing to do.

>> My last trip to the castle, I didn't have to fight the
>> soldiers in the barracks until after I had the wand.

In my case, they're usually woken by a lich ("You feel that monsters are
aware of your presence") and released from the barracks by (I guess) a
giant knocking down the door.

>> As for being a match for you, no single monster that you might
>> see in Gehennom at XL14 should be a problem except a named
>> demon, minotaur, jabberwock, ki-rin, purple lich or (m)mf.

> Well, yah, that's a sufficeintly long exception list.  I've been to
> Gehennom before.  I just haven't been to Ludios or on the Arc's quest.

Actually, it's quite a short list. You will rarely encounter even one of
those, and the only real problems are the named demon (but you would
recognise those from a distance and stop your decent there if you
weren't sure of yourself) and the purple liches. The other ones listed
cannot teleport, so they are "harmless" in a maze.

> And doesn't jumping in water risk drowning?

No, but you should only do it at a "safe" place (no monsters nearby, and
no places out of sight close where new creatures may generate), since it
involves taking off rustable items and dropping items that may get
otherwise damaged.

If you jump into water, *next to the shore*, you will crawl out again.
You will drown if you (for example) take off a ring of levitation above
a wet spot surrounded by water.

